.TH GPU 3
.SH NAME
gpu \- graphics devices for user-space drivers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #G /dev

.B #G/0/ctl
.B #G/0/irq
.B #G/0/dirty
 ...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I gpu
device is the kernel's whole part of a graphics driver: it hands
the hardware of every PCI display controller (class 03) to a
user-space driver, such as
.IR virtiogpu
in
.IR gpu (4).
It knows nothing about mode setting, memory management, command
submission or firmware.
.PP
Each controller has a directory
.IR n ,
numbered from 0 in PCI order, with three files.
.PP
Reading
.B ctl
returns lines of text:
.TF "bar\fIi\fP \fIaddr size seg\fP"
.TP
.BI pci " bus.dev.fn"
the device, as named in
.BR #$/pci ;
its configuration space is
.BI #$/pci/ bus.dev.fn raw\f1.
.TP
.BI id " vid/did
vendor and device id.
.TP
.BI irq " line state count
the interrupt line, whether
.B irq
has enabled it, and the interrupts seen.
.TP
.BI bar i " addr size seg \fR[\fPcseg\fR]\fP
a base address register: physical address, size and the physical
segment that maps it, uncached (see
.IR segattach (2)),
.B -
for an I/O bar; after
.BI "bar " i " cached"
also the cached segment.
.TP
.BI fb " addr size seg
the framebuffer, if allocated, else
.BR "fb none" .
.PD
.PP
The memory bars are attachable segments named
.BI gpu n bar i\f1,
uncached; the framebuffer is
.BI gpu n fb\f1.
.PP
Writing
.B ctl
(by the host owner) controls the device:
.TF "fb \fIbytes\fP"
.TP
.B "bme on\fR|\fPoff
enable or disable bus mastering.
.TP
.BI fb " bytes
allocate a physically contiguous framebuffer of at least
.I bytes
bytes, once; later requests that fit are no-ops.
.TP
.B "irq ack
unmask the interrupt (see below).
.TP
.BI bar " n " cached
also map memory bar
.I n
write-back, as the segment
.BI gpu n bar n c\f1,
and make the MTRRs agree; only for memory that is RAM to the
processor, such as virtio-gpu's host visible memory, which only the
driver knows.
.PD
.PP
Opening
.B irq
enables the device's legacy (INTx) interrupt.
A read blocks until the device interrupts and returns the count.
The kernel's handler masks the interrupt with the interrupt-disable
bit of the PCI command register; the driver serves the device and
writes
.B "irq ack
to unmask it.
.PP
.B dirty
connects the framebuffer to
.IR draw (3):
with
.IR vga (3)
type
.BR gpu ,
the screen is the framebuffer of controller 0, drawn into directly,
and every flush of the screen is collected here.
A read blocks until something changed and returns the bounding
rectangle as
.IB "x0 y0 x1 y1" .
The driver copies that part to the display.
.SH EXAMPLES
What the driver does to show the screen:
.EX
	echo fb 9216000 >'#G/0/ctl'
	echo type gpu >/dev/vgactl
	echo size 1280x800x32 x8r8g8b8 >/dev/vgactl
	echo drawinit >/dev/vgactl
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/9/port/devgpu.c
.br
.B /sys/src/9/pc/vgagpu.c
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR gpu (4),
.IR segment (3),
.IR vga (3),
.IR segattach (2)
.SH BUGS
Only the first controller's framebuffer can be the screen.
.br
Only legacy interrupts; no MSI.
.br
The framebuffer is never freed.
.br
The kernel's console keeps writing on the screen.
